#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dec21a9-eec8-5e39-9af6-5ba355a11c5b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "44c87021-177d-5f5c-b1f9-47eb08f107a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4146" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ if $\\ln(4x+5)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+5) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+5)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln(4x+5)} = 4x+5.$ \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+5 & ={e}^{3} \\\\ 4x & ={e}^{3}-5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae88a0b-9927-573b-a072-d0aa1861a9cd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "0cfb7d21-6ca4-55a3-a46a-7a765ed1b866" :question/math-academy-id "e-4147" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to $3\\ln (5x+1)-2 = 13.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)-2 & =13 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e,$ so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =5 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)} & ={e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln (5x+1)} = 5x+1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+1 & ={e}^{5} \\\\ 5x & ={e}^{5}-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{5}-1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c27840-76fa-5c01-98cc-587dc063f19c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "85f82353-2436-5e48-984a-5e671ee54024" :question/math-academy-id "e-4148" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ such that $\\ln(1-2x) + 5\\ln(1-2x) = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =4 \\\\ (1+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =4 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e,$ so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x)} & ={e}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln(1-2x)} = 1-2x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2x & ={e}^{2/3} \\\\ 1-2x & =\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}} \\\\ -2x & =\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}-1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{-\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}+1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1-\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae88a0b-9927-573b-a072-d0aa1861a9cd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "128bee6c-0558-5d9a-a5b5-9554b30291f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525246} {:answer/id #uuid "6c328adf-e222-53df-8b0b-b5bead56d410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525482} {:answer/id #uuid "8919c346-a935-556e-b345-b1456ce9de4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525550} {:answer/id #uuid "b029dcaa-201b-5b47-8796-2ac35fb685de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525651} {:answer/id #uuid "e68b99f9-6dbc-53d2-98c1-ec15610745d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8919c346-a935-556e-b345-b1456ce9de4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525550} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee666b6-cdff-57bc-9fb7-d7297eacc588" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a62752b-0746-55f7-b956-62f3d4e94841" :question/math-academy-id "q-110776" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1 & =2 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & ={e}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}=x+1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={e}^{1/2} \\\\ x+1 & =\\sqrt{e} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{e}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c27840-76fa-5c01-98cc-587dc063f19c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1ba94f-f45c-512c-b1d1-f003b8f8dfe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525239} {:answer/id #uuid "34ba3e37-2b18-5100-b41a-9a41e2697f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525338} {:answer/id #uuid "4dee1f41-59b2-5b1b-b30b-46402dbb4b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525402} {:answer/id #uuid "aa216821-4207-55ec-8590-464c6c397b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525636} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d9ab0d-677a-5197-bcc6-518af361811f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34ba3e37-2b18-5100-b41a-9a41e2697f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525338} :answer-field/id #uuid "708c5f6b-a9d2-551b-9566-d442919930e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5e20f20-51b5-534a-8fa4-4e59ce4c58da" :question/math-academy-id "q-110778" :question/problem "If $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=7$, then what is the value of  $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =7 \\\\ (2+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =7 \\\\ 7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, $x=e.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dec21a9-eec8-5e39-9af6-5ba355a11c5b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e631112-94a1-5d36-a4a3-24d0effe4f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{1/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525235} {:answer/id #uuid "2fab8c5f-a89b-5874-94ad-d43e4e9e77cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525318} {:answer/id #uuid "b4395f11-17c4-52bc-ab71-bb05b4766460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525669} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb3075a-48b5-53b7-98be-13883035108d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525799} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2b2919-4a66-5852-8746-64b6bfbcbd32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeb3075a-48b5-53b7-98be-13883035108d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525799} :answer-field/id #uuid "56827e89-7d4e-5ed2-b123-3e520ac5f54c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49019dd1-55bb-562f-a7d6-f6148b65a5e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-19175" :question/problem "Find $x$ such that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)=5.$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x) & =5 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)} & ={e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}=5x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ={e}^{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dec21a9-eec8-5e39-9af6-5ba355a11c5b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93c6d489-fdcb-554a-8052-9aa3abc483a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525582} {:answer/id #uuid "deeee168-068f-54a5-a0c7-a932832e533c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525757} {:answer/id #uuid "f158dabb-3e3a-5810-a8fe-d95d5b34eb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525806} {:answer/id #uuid "f3689c71-5472-5410-a8d8-755ef04120b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525814} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b6718d-3797-5931-b79d-8070aacd4951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3-e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93c6d489-fdcb-554a-8052-9aa3abc483a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525582} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f247dc-e2c5-5442-a20f-ad6c40fb8104" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "151a655e-4d18-5ef1-b8cd-5631f4184ad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-19181" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/3)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/3)}=\\frac{x}{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & ={e}^{3} \\\\ x & =3{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c27840-76fa-5c01-98cc-587dc063f19c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8826f4c0-5a42-59a2-9151-b35e6ad20bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109940} {:answer/id #uuid "3de17066-fadf-5f18-b644-223597f74318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109942} {:answer/id #uuid "5c270b95-b18d-51d3-bd3a-4c9f0a72b267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109944} {:answer/id #uuid "132b05f3-4e37-54c7-b862-8d9ced0267da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525247} {:answer/id #uuid "96a26908-0104-5b60-a997-3d51a9bf0d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3de17066-fadf-5f18-b644-223597f74318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109942} :answer-field/id #uuid "b43822f8-5c74-5fd7-8419-7849b452203b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4428092c-dd91-56c8-97be-17f43bd92085" :question/math-academy-id "q-66748" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$, then what is the value of  $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ (1+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{1/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{1/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae88a0b-9927-573b-a072-d0aa1861a9cd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2741bb37-721a-541f-ae38-d2c73903721d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3+{e}^{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525301} {:answer/id #uuid "303bb5ce-15fd-5cca-bb99-efee1d2af9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-{e}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525322} {:answer/id #uuid "4864dc9f-3595-5120-a1c9-e719a053ee11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{e}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525387} {:answer/id #uuid "59514e66-3df6-513f-aa2c-b396cf998179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525435} {:answer/id #uuid "fffa625d-8f6a-528d-8c8e-e01b69da0042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fffa625d-8f6a-528d-8c8e-e01b69da0042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525846} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7509eb2-c256-551d-a0b2-6e744478a267" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33c6eec8-2b71-56e5-96cd-913d5dc6b9ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-79393" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)+3=9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)+3 & =9 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x) & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)}=1-5x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-5x & ={e}^{3} \\\\ -5x & ={e}^{3}-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{-5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae88a0b-9927-573b-a072-d0aa1861a9cd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb6d139-a467-507e-859b-2bf644eeaf69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525355} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae28c93-a657-5502-963c-83b06539b141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24-{e}^{4}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525391} {:answer/id #uuid "6576e444-158f-57e8-8d0a-1dc86cacadf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-{e}^{4}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525464} {:answer/id #uuid "80c88710-0619-57c3-9a8c-ce38f82b313b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7-{e}^{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525528} {:answer/id #uuid "96536e6a-0835-50e3-9eee-75ad45f23eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-24}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6576e444-158f-57e8-8d0a-1dc86cacadf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-{e}^{4}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525464} :answer-field/id #uuid "90195487-fcb1-57cc-8d0f-33b1c9fdb8ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bba3adf3-06bf-565a-834f-7b41da6bd21b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7940" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to $4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)+8=24.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)+8 & =24 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x) & =16 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x) & =4 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)} & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)}=3-7x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-7x & ={e}^{4} \\\\ -7x & ={e}^{4}-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{4}-3}{-7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3-{e}^{4}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
